According to Gauss law of magnetic field the flux of magnetic field across a closed loop is zero.
It is given as;
$\int \vec{B} \cdot \overrightarrow{d s}=0$

Length of rod, $l=1.0 \mathrm{ m} \begin{aligned} B=0.25 \mathrm{ T}, f & =12 \mathrm{rev}/ \mathrm{s}\\
\therefore \quad \text{ Induced emf, } e & =\frac{B \omega l^2}{2}=\frac{0.25 \times 2 \pi f}{2} \times1^2 \\ \text{$ and $} =0.25 \pi f=0.25 \pi \times12 \\ \text{ and } =3 \pi=9.42 \mathrm{ V}
\end{aligned}$

Magnitude of induced current in the wire $A B$ is given as

I=$\frac{B v l \sin \theta}{R}$Here,

$\begin{aligned}$& $\theta=$90^{{}^{\circ}} \\
& I=$\frac{B v l}{R}
\end{aligned}

\therefore$When wire $A B$ is replaced by semicircular wire, then neither $l$ nor $\theta$ are changed. $B, v and R$ are already constant. Hence, induced current will remains same.

Given,
$\begin{aligned}
L=50 \mathrm{mH}$& =5 \times 10^{-2} $\mathrm{H}\\
I & =4 \mathrm{~A}
\end{aligned}$\(\therefore\) Energy stored in $the inductor is given as(E=\frac{1}{2}L$I^2=$\frac{1}{2} \times$5 \times 10^{-2} \times 4^2=0.4 $\mathrm{~J}$
